\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Prompt: 
\f1 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 what influences success of VBAC (vaginal birth after caesarian)
\f0 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
***\
VBAC success depends on a mix of obstetric history, current pregnancy factors, and labour characteristics. The main influences:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Strongest positive predictors
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Prior vaginal birth
\f0\b0 , especially a prior successful VBAC \'97 this is the single most powerful predictor, pushing success rates above 85\'9690%.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Spontaneous onset of labour
\f0\b0  rather than induction.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Favourable cervix at admission
\f0\b0  (higher Bishop score).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Previous caesarean for a non-recurring indication
\f0\b0  (e.g. breech, fetal distress) rather than a recurring one like failure to progress or cephalopelvic disproportion.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Negative predictors
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Induction or augmentation of labour
\f0\b0 , particularly with prostaglandins (which also raise uterine rupture risk).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Maternal obesity
\f0\b0  (BMI >30, and especially >40).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advanced maternal age
\f0\b0  (>40).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Short inter-delivery interval
\f0\b0  (<18\'9624 months from the previous caesarean).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Recurring indication
\f0\b0  for the previous caesarean, especially arrest of dilation in the active phase.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Macrosomia
\f0\b0  (estimated fetal weight >4000g, and more so >4500g).\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Gestational age >40 weeks
\f0\b0 .\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Preeclampsia or other pregnancy complications
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Other factors
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Type of prior uterine incision
\f0\b0  matters more for safety than success \'97 low transverse is the standard for attempting VBAC; classical or T-incisions are contraindications.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Number of prior caesareans
\f0\b0 : success rates are similar after one or two prior caesareans, though rupture risk rises.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ethnicity and socioeconomic factors
\f0\b0  appear in some prediction models, though interpretation is contested and likely reflects access-to-care effects rather than biology.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 Prediction tools
\f0\b0  The MFMU (Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units) Network calculator from Grobman et al. is the most validated tool, combining maternal age, BMI, ethnicity, prior vaginal delivery, prior VBAC, and indication for prior caesarean. A 2021 revision removed race/ethnicity from the model after concerns about how those variables were being applied clinically. Baseline success rates for a planned VBAC ("trial of labour after caesarean", TOLAC) sit around 60\'9680% across unselected populations.\
Is this for a specific clinical decision, a research question, or general interest? I can go deeper on the evidence base or the rupture-risk side if useful.\
